  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 154 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nNew Mexico will remain sandwiched between a ridge off to the east \nand troughing to the west over the weekend. However, the monsoonal \nmoisture plume will become notably thinner, with storm coverage \nfocusing along the plume. Current model guidance suggests this could \nbe along the central mountain chain, which would be advantageous for \nthe development of storms that will have a tendency to regenerate \nover the high terrain. Of particular concern will be the Ruidoso \narea burn scars, although other locations along and near the central \nmountain chain will be at risk as well. The center of the H5 will \nattempt to re-develop over central NM Sunday, slowing down storm \nmotions and increasing temps. On Sunday, LREF 500 mb heights are \naround 595 dam over central NM, but some deterministic models have \nit as strong as 598 dam. Storm coverage will depend on how quickly \nheights rise, with higher heights signifying more mid-level warming \nand therefore less afternoon destabilization. Moisture will drop \ndown to near seasonal normals in most areas Sunday into Monday, \nexcept across the northern third of the state north of the building \nridge. Rain chances will be highest along the east slopes of the \nSangre de Cristo Mountains and this pattern should persist into the \nmiddle of the week, with the same areas getting rain each \nafternoon/evening. \n\nForecast confidence becomes low late next week. Cluster analysis \nshows that the center of the H5 ridge could be located anywhere from \nthe Great Basin to Texas and anywhere in between. The wide range of \nsolutions suggests anywhere from isolated to numerous storms and \ntemperatures anywhere from near normal to well-above normal.",
